      "output": "**Assumption:** You are a mechanical\u2011engineering student preparing for a thermodynamics final on the 20th and want a focused two\u2011week review schedule.\n\n**Two\u2011week study plan (Day\u202f1\u202f\u2013\u202fDay\u202f14)**  \n\n| Day | Focus | Tasks (\u22482\u202fh total) |\n|-----|-------|-------------------|\n| 1 | Foundations | Review lecture notes on the First Law; redo the three example problems at the end of the chapter. |\n| 2 | Energy balances | Watch a 15\u2011min video on steady\u2011flow systems; solve the two textbook problems on open\u2011system energy balance. |\n| 3 | Property tables | Practice reading steam\u2011table entries; complete the \u201centhalpy vs. temperature\u201d worksheet. |\n| 4 | Second Law \u2013 concepts | Summarize entropy generation in your own words; work the two Carnot\u2011cycle problems. |\n| 5 | Entropy calculations | Solve three problems involving entropy change of ideal gases (isothermal, adiabatic, polytropic). |\n| 6 | Review & quiz | Create a one\u2011page mind map of Laws\u202f1\u20132; take a 20\u2011question self\u2011quiz (no notes). |\n| 7 | Power cycles | Study the Rankine cycle diagram; compute net work and efficiency for a given set of data. |\n| 8 | Refrigeration | Derive the COP formula; solve the two textbook refrigeration\u2011cycle problems. |\n| 9 | Combustion & chemical energy | Review combustion\u2011energy basics; balance a simple hydrocarbon combustion and calculate heat release. |\n|10 | Mixed\u2011practice | Do a mixed\u2011problem set (5\u202fquestions) covering all topics studied so far; time yourself (30\u202fmin). |\n|11 | Weak\u2011spot focus | Identify the two problems you missed on Day\u202f10; re\u2011work them with detailed steps. |\n|12 | Past exam analysis | Locate a previous thermodynamics exam (or sample); attempt the first 3 problems under timed conditions. |\n|13 | Full mock exam | Simulate exam conditions: 2\u202fh for a 5\u2011question set covering all chapters. |\n|14 | Review & relax | Go over any remaining errors; create a \u201ccheat sheet\u201d of key formulas and concepts; take a short break to reset before the final. |\n\n**Next step you can start today:** Gather your lecture slides, textbook chapter outlines, and any past exams you have access to, and set up a dedicated study space for the 2\u2011hour blocks.\n\n**Check:** Which thermodynamics topic do you feel least confident about right now?",
